 ],
 "literal": "The drum with a full voice beaten by an excellent drumstick, / making the orderly land submit to a single command, / sounding like roaring thunder that strikes snakes, / even those of great status who ruled alone, / established their fame and departed, perishing indeed. / Therefore, O son of the wise one! O chief of warriors! / ... please listen as I speak. / Without others knowing your strength/secrets, / discerning the words spoken by others, / dedicating the daytime of the sun to active duties, / seeking what comes in the limit of the night, / word ... / like a great bull that has finished plowing and eats the straw, / mingling with red-eyed women in small drops, / as they pour beautiful clarified wine into vessels, / of imperishable wealth ... / without any delay to those who desire food, / graciously granting cooked rice to those who desire it, / slaughtering rams and carving the roasted meat, / on the banks of long sand near the swollen water reservoirs, / in every grove ... / death is a reality, it is not an illusion indeed!",
 "translation": "Even the great rulers of the past, who held the entire orderly earth under their single command, announced by the loud roar of drums beaten with sturdy drumsticks—drums that sounded like the thunder that strikes down serpents—have departed this world, leaving only their fame behind. Therefore, O son of the wise, chief of brave warriors, listen to my words! Keep your plans secret from others, but understand clearly what others say. Spend the hours of the sun in active duty, and plan for what is to come during the night. Like a great bull that rests after plowing and feeds on straw, enjoy yourself in the company of red-eyed women, mingling with them as they pour fine, clear wine from vessels. To those who ask for food, give generously without delay; slaughter rams and carve the roasted meat for them. Feast in every grove along the sandy banks of the wide water reservoirs. For death is an absolute certainty; it is no illusion!",
 "modern": "சிறந்த குச்சியால் அடிக்கப்பட்ட, உரத்த ஓசையுடைய முரசு, ஒழுங்குடைய உலகத்தை ஒரே ஆணையின் கீழ் கொண்டுவர, பாம்புகளைத் தாக்கும் இடியைப் போல முழங்க, உலகை ஆண்ட ஒப்பற்ற பெருமை உடையவர்களும் தங்கள் புகழை நிலைநிறுத்திவிட்டு இறந்துபோனார்கள். அதனால், அறிவுடையோனின் மகனே! வீரர்களின் தலைவனே! நான் கூறுவதைக் கேட்பாயாக. உன்னுடைய வலிமையையும் திட்டங்களையும் பிறர் அறியாதவாறு காத்து, பிறர் கூறும் சொற்களை ஆராய்ந்து அறிவாயாக. பகல் பொழுதை உனது கடமைகளுக்காகப் பயன்படுத்தி, இரவில் வரவிருப்பதை ஆராய்ந்து திட்டமிடுவாயாக. உழுது முடித்த பெரிய எருது வைக்கோலைத் தின்பது போல, செங்கண் மகளிரோடு கூடி மகிழ்ந்து, அவர்கள் பாத்திரங்களில் ஊற்றும் தெளிந்த மதுவை அருந்துவாயாக. அழியாத செல்வத்தை உடையவனே! உணவு வேண்டுவோருக்குத் தடையின்றி அளித்து, சோறு வேண்டுவோருக்குக் கொடுத்து, ஆடுகளை அறுத்து அவற்றின் சுட்ட கறியைப் பகிர்ந்து கொடுப்பாயாக. நீர் நிறைந்த நீர்நிலைகளின் மணல் நிறைந்த கரைகளில் உள்ள சோலைகள் தோறும் மகிழ்ந்திருப்பாயாக. ஏனெனில், மரணம் என்பது உண்மை; அது பொய் அல்ல!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai and the Peruṅkāñci tuṟai, which focuses on the transience of life and the inevitability of death. The poet Kōtamanār addresses Tarumaputtiraṉ, advising him on how to live a meaningful life in the face of mortality. He begins by reminding the king that even the greatest emperors who ruled the entire world with absolute power have perished, leaving only their fame behind. The poet advises the king to perform his duties diligently during the day and plan strategically at night, while keeping his own counsel secret. He uses the vivid image of a hardworking bull resting and eating straw to suggest that the king should also enjoy life's pleasures, such as fine wine and the company of women, after fulfilling his duties. Finally, he urges the king to practice immense generosity by feeding the needy and hosting grand feasts, emphasizing that death is an absolute reality and not an illusion.",
